  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 143 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nToday through Tonight...\nEarly morning surface analysis depicts a low-pressure system \npositioned over Ontario north of Lake Superior, trailing a cold \nfront south through Wisconsin. Across central Illinois, a broad \ncol area remains sandwiched between surface high pressure along \nthe East Coast and a secondary ridge building across the Great \nPlains. Given limited moisture return and stronger forcing \nstaying displaced well north, frontal precipitation will remain \nconfined to near and north of the IL/WI state line today. \nAbundant insolation and dry low levels will foster deep \natmospheric mixing this afternoon, pushing high temperatures \ninto the mid-to-upper 70s under mostly sunny skies.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...\nThe surface ridge axis across the Plains will migrate eastward \ninto the Midwest on Monday. Northwest flow in the wake of the \ndry cold front will result in weak cold air advection into the \nregion, trimming temperatures back toward seasonal norms. Expect\nhighs on Monday ranging from the upper 60s near and north of \nI-74 to low 70s southward. Mid-level ridging and southerly low-\nlevel return flow quickly re-establish by Tuesday and Wednesday \nas the high shifts east. This pattern will initiate a warming \ntrend, with afternoon highs climbing into the mid-to-upper 70s \non Tuesday and reaching the upper 70s to lower 80s by Wednesday.\n\nThursday through Next Weekend...\nAn extended period of tranquil and warm conditions is\nanticipated to persist through late week and into the weekend \nunder a broad upper level ridge. While light ambient winds \nshould keep critical fire weather threats low, the combination \nof minimal precipitation over the coming week and low relative \nhumidity values will continue to dry out agricultural fine \nfuels. As fall harvest operations peak, extra caution is \nrecommended to avoid equipment related fire sparks in fields.",
